  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery outlined remaining midlevel clouds\nslowly dissolving across western zones. Midlevel shortwave\ntrough axis was in the process of clearing the eastern CWA, and\nexpect the clearing process to accelerate this afternoon as\nsubsidence increases. A chilly lower atmosphere remains, with\n850 mb temperatures just below 0C, so temperatures will struggle\ninto the lower 40s this afternoon even with increasing sunshine. \n\nIn the dry subsident regime with light winds, another widescale\nhard freeze will occur tonight and Wednesday morning, with lower\n20s common. \n\nClosed midlevel cyclone dives south near Kansas City Wednesday,\nwith some clouds kicking into the eastern zones on the back side\nof the cyclone's circulation. No other impacts expected. Despite\nthe flow, models show modest net warming of +2-4C at 850 mb,\nallowing temperatures to climb further into the 40s. Yet another\nhard freeze in the lower 20s Thanksgiving morning, courtesy of \n1032 mb surface high centered near the eastern zones 6 am.\nRadiational cooling looks ideal across northeast zones, and will\nlikely see some teens sunrise Thanksgiving northeast of Dodge\nCity. Noticeably milder temperatures, near 60, are expected\nBlack Friday afternoon. \n\nSW KS will remain dry, with high confidence, through daylight \nSaturday. Excellent holiday travel conditions will result. The \nnext significant cold front is apparent this weekend, but models\nare out of alignment regarding its strength, timing and any \nprecipitation potential. Low grade NBM pops for the northeast \ncounties Saturday night are justified, pending how amplified the\nwave is when it crosses Kansas this weekend. Regardless,\ntemperatures should return closer to normal Sunday and Monday.",
